      <div class="tg-note">
        <span class="tg-note__label">What This Part Is For</span>
        Two mistakes are made at the start of nearly every assurance argument. The
        first is to assume the Old Testament taught salvation by works and the New
        Testament corrected it — which makes the OT irrelevant to the question and
        also makes God's method change halfway through his own book. The second is
        to assume the tension between comfort and warning is a problem created by
        systematic theologians. It is not. It is in Ezekiel. It is in Deuteronomy.
        It is in the single verse of Habakkuk that the New Testament quotes three
        times. Before we can weigh the competing verses in Part II, we need to see
        that the competition is native to Scripture and that Scripture holds it
        without embarrassment.
      </div>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 1: WHAT "SAVED" MEANS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="question">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">What "Saved" Means in the Old Testament</h2>

        <p>The Hebrew verb behind most of our "save" language is
        <em>yasha'</em> (יָשַׁע) — to bring into a wide place, to rescue from a
        constriction. Its noun forms give us <em>yeshu'ah</em>, salvation, and the
        name <em>Yeshua</em>. The picture is not primarily courtroom acquittal; it
        is a person hemmed in and then given room.</p>

        <p>In the Old Testament that rescue is overwhelmingly concrete: from Egypt,
        from enemies, from famine, from the pit, from death. This has led some
        readers to conclude that the Old Testament knows nothing of what we mean by
        salvation. That conclusion moves too fast. The concrete rescues are told in
        such a way that they interpret one another and build toward something
        larger, and the Old Testament itself says so — the psalmist who cries
        "restore to me the joy of your salvation"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 51:12">Psalm 51:12</a>) is not asking to be
        pulled out of a well.</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">yasha' / yeshu'ah</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">יָשַׁע · יְשׁוּעָה</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">To be delivered out of a narrow, pressing
          place into open ground; the deliverance itself. Used of military rescue,
          of legal vindication for the poor, of healing, of restoration from exile,
          and — increasingly through the Psalms and Isaiah — of a rescue from sin
          and death that only God can accomplish and that has not yet fully
          arrived. The word's trajectory across the canon is one of the clearer
          arguments that the Testaments are telling one story.</span>
        </div>

        <p>Alongside it sits the question this study is actually about: not only
        <em>are these people rescued?</em> but <em>are these people God's?</em> The
        Old Testament's word for that relationship is covenant, and covenant is where
        both the comfort and the warning live.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 2: EDEN
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="eden">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Eden: God Moves First</h2>

        <p>The first thing that happens after the first sin is that God comes
        walking and calls out, <em>"Where are you?"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 3:9">Genesis 3:9</a>). The question is not
        for God's information. It is the opening of a search.</p>

        <p>Three things are established in Genesis 3 that never afterward change.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">One</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The initiative is God's</div>
          <p>Adam and Eve hide; God seeks. Nothing in the narrative suggests they
          were moving toward him. The pattern of the whole canon — a God who comes
          looking for people who are going the other way — is set in the first three
          chapters and is never reversed.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Two</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The remedy is provided, not achieved</div>
          <p>They sew fig leaves; the covering does not hold. God makes them
          garments of skin (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 3:21">Genesis 3:21</a>).
          Christian readers have long heard a first sacrifice in that verse, and the
          text does not say so explicitly — but it does say plainly that the covering
          humans made for themselves was replaced by a covering God made for them.
          That much is on the page.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Three</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Consequence is not cancelled</div>
          <p>They are clothed and they are exiled. The garden is shut. Mercy and
          judgment arrive in the same paragraph and neither one softens the other.
          Anyone who wants a Bible in which grace means the removal of consequences
          has to get past Genesis 3 first, and no one has.</p>
        </div>

        <p>This third point matters more for our question than it may seem. A very
        large amount of assurance anxiety comes from an unstated assumption that if
        God's people can suffer real loss, they must not really be God's. Genesis
        answers that before the question is asked.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 3: ABRAHAM
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="abraham">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Abraham: The Verse the Whole Argument Runs Through</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          And he believed the LORD, and he counted it to him as righteousness.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 15:6">Genesis 15:6</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Everything in the later debate is compressed into this sentence, which is
        why both Paul and James reach for it and why they appear to pull it in
        opposite directions.</p>

        <p>The verb translated "believed" is <em>he'emin</em> — the causative of
        <em>'aman</em> (אמן), the root behind <em>amen</em>. Its core sense is
        firmness, reliability, being solidly supported. To "believe" God in Hebrew is
        to rest one's weight on him. The verb translated "counted" is
        <em>hashav</em>, a bookkeeping word: to reckon something to an account.</p>

        <div class="tg-definition">
          <span class="tg-definition__term">'emunah</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__original">אֱמוּנָה</span>
          <span class="tg-definition__def">The noun from the same root. English has
          to choose between "faith" and "faithfulness"; Hebrew does not, because it
          does not regard them as two things. <em>'Emunah</em> is steadiness — the
          quality of a thing that holds when leaned on. It is used of Moses' hands
          held up until sunset (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 17:12">Exodus
          17:12</a>) and of God's own reliability
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 32:4">Deuteronomy 32:4</a>). This
          single fact about Hebrew vocabulary does more to dissolve the
          faith-versus-works impasse than most of the arguments built on top of
          it.</span>
        </div>

        <p>Note what Genesis 15:6 does <em>not</em> say. It does not say Abraham was
        righteous. It says righteousness was <em>credited</em> to him, on the basis
        of trust, in a context where he had just been told to count stars he could
        not count and promised a son he did not have. Nothing had been performed.
        The ledger entry precedes the obedience.</p>

        <p>Paul builds on that sequence with two observations the text supports
        exactly. First, the crediting happens in Genesis 15; circumcision is not
        commanded until Genesis 17 — so Abraham was reckoned righteous while still
        uncircumcised (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 4:9-12">Romans 4:9–12</a>).
        Second, the law comes 430 years later, and a later contract cannot annul an
        earlier promise (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 3:17">Galatians
        3:17</a>). These are not creative readings; they are chronological facts
        about the Genesis narrative.</p>

        <p>James builds on the same verse from the other end
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 2:21-24">James 2:21–24</a>), pointing out
        that Genesis 15:6 is quoted again at the binding of Isaac — the point at
        which Abraham's trust was demonstrated under maximum pressure — and
        concluding that the faith which was credited was the kind of faith that
        eventually walked up a mountain with a knife.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Two Apostles Are Answering Two Questions</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Paul asks: <em>on what basis is a person
          accepted?</em> James asks: <em>how do you tell whether the thing a person
          calls faith is faith?</em></p>
          <p>Both answer from Abraham because Abraham's life contains both moments —
          the crediting in chapter 15 and the proof in chapter 22, separated by
          decades and by a great deal of failure in between. Paul's opponent is the
          man who thinks he must earn standing. James' opponent is the man who says
          he has faith and has never once acted like it. Neither is arguing with the
          other, and the tradition has spent a great deal of energy inventing a
          quarrel that the texts themselves do not have.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Hold on to one detail for Part IV: <strong>the decades in between.</strong>
        Between the crediting and the demonstration, Abraham lies about his wife
        twice, tries to secure the promise through Hagar, and laughs at God. The man
        the New Testament calls the father of the faithful had a track record that
        would fail most modern assurance checklists. That is not a marginal
        observation; it is data about what "faith that works" actually looks like
        over a life.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 4: EXODUS GRAMMAR
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="exodus">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Exodus Grammar — Rescue Before Law</h2>

        <p>The exodus is the Old Testament's master picture of salvation, and its
        structure is unmistakable once you look for it. Israel is not rescued because
        they are obedient. They are not even especially willing — they complain
        before the sea and after it. They are rescued because God heard, remembered
        his covenant with Abraham, and acted
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 2:24">Exodus 2:24</a>).</p>

        <p>The law is then given to a people <em>already</em> redeemed, and the Ten
        Commandments say so in their opening line before a single command is
        issued:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          I am the LORD your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of
          the house of slavery.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 20:2">Exodus 20:2</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The sentence is the grammar of the whole Bible in miniature. The
        indicative comes first — <em>this is who I am and what I have done for
        you</em> — and the imperatives follow from it. Obedience is not the price of
        rescue; it is the shape of a rescued life. Every New Testament epistle is
        built the same way, which is why Romans 1–11 precedes Romans 12 and Ephesians
        1–3 precedes Ephesians 4.</p>

        <div class="tg-timeline">
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Ex 2:24</span><span>God <strong>remembers the covenant</strong> — the cause of the rescue is God's prior promise, not Israel's condition</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Ex 12</span><span><strong>Blood applied</strong> — the household is spared not by its merit but by a mark it did not invent; the lamb dies in the house's place</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Ex 14</span><span><strong>"Stand still and see"</strong> — the sea is crossed by a people who contributed nothing but walking</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Ex 20</span><span><strong>Law given</strong> — to people already out, already free, already his</span></div>
          <div class="tg-timeline__row"><span class="tg-timeline__date">Ex 32</span><span><strong>Golden calf</strong> — forty days later. The redeemed people apostatise almost immediately, and the covenant survives by intercession</span></div>
        </div>

        <p>That last row is the one usually skipped, and it is essential to this
        study. Within six weeks of the most spectacular deliverance in the Old
        Testament, the delivered people are worshipping a metal animal. The covenant
        is not maintained by their consistency. It is maintained because Moses stands
        in the breach and because God is "merciful and gracious, slow to anger"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 34:6">Exodus 34:6</a>) — a self-description
        given <em>in response to</em> the golden calf, not in spite of it.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Two Things Both True at Once</span>
          Exodus 32 records both that God does not destroy the nation <em>and</em>
          that three thousand men die that day. The chapter refuses to be used for
          either "sin does not matter to a redeemed people" or "sin ends the
          relationship." It is the earliest full statement of the tension this whole
          study is about, and it comes with no resolution attached — only a mediator
          standing between.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 5: SINAI AND "IF"
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="sinai">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Sinai and the Word <em>If</em></h2>

        <p>Having established that rescue precedes law, honesty requires the other
        half. The Sinai covenant is drenched in conditions.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Now therefore, <strong>if</strong> you will indeed obey my voice and keep
          my covenant, you shall be my treasured possession among all peoples.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 19:5">Exodus 19:5</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 28">Deuteronomy 28</a> spends
        fourteen verses on blessings for obedience and fifty-four on curses for
        disobedience — a ratio that is itself a sermon. Deuteronomy 30 puts the
        choice in the plainest possible terms: <em>"I have set before you life and
        death, blessing and curse. Therefore choose life"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 30:19">Deuteronomy 30:19</a>).</p>

        <p>Any account of assurance that cannot accommodate this material is not
        accounting for the Bible. God's covenant people were told, repeatedly, at
        length, with specifics, that continuing in the covenant's blessings depended
        on their covenant loyalty — and the rest of the Old Testament is the record
        of those curses arriving.</p>

        <p>And yet. Inside the same chapter of Deuteronomy that issues the choice,
        this is said:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          And the LORD your God will circumcise your heart and the heart of your
          offspring, so that you will love the LORD your God with all your heart and
          with all your soul, that you may live.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 30:6">Deuteronomy 30:6</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Read the sequence of Deuteronomy 30 as it stands. Verses 1–5 assume the
        curses have already fallen and the people are scattered. Verse 6 promises
        that God will perform surgery on the organ of loyalty itself. Verses 11–14
        insist the commandment is not too hard or too far away. Verses 15–20 then
        set the choice.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Condition and the Enabling Are in the Same Speech</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Deuteronomy does not choose between "you
          must love God" and "God will make you love God." It says both, in order,
          without apology.</p>
          <p>This is the single most important structural fact in the Old Testament
          for our question, and it recurs at every level of the canon. The command
          is real and the failure to keep it is fatal; the ability to keep it is a
          gift God promises to give. Systems that flatten this into one clause — in
          either direction — are tidier than Deuteronomy and less true to it.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 6: WILDERNESS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="wilderness">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Wilderness Generation — Scripture's Own Warning Passage</h2>

        <p>Of all the Old Testament material bearing on assurance, one episode is
        singled out by the New Testament more than any other: the generation that
        came out of Egypt and did not enter Canaan.</p>

        <p>Their case is the hard one on purpose. They were not outsiders. They came
        through the sea, ate the manna, drank from the rock, saw the fire on the
        mountain. Whatever "belonging to the covenant people" means, they had it — and
        of the adults who left Egypt, only Caleb and Joshua crossed the Jordan
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 14:29-30">Numbers 14:29–30</a>).</p>

        <p>Paul reads the episode as directly applicable to the church, and the way
        he sets it up is striking:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Our fathers were all under the cloud, and all passed through the sea, and
          all were baptized into Moses in the cloud and in the sea, and all ate the
          same spiritual food, and all drank the same spiritual drink... Nevertheless,
          with most of them God was not pleased, for they were overthrown in the
          wilderness. Now these things took place as examples for us... Therefore let
          anyone who thinks that he stands take heed lest he fall.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 10:1-12">1 Corinthians 10:1–12</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Paul stacks the word "all" five times to make the point that full
        participation in the covenant signs was not the issue. Then he says
        <em>these things happened as examples for us</em> — for the Corinthian
        church, who had also been baptised and also ate a covenant meal.</p>

        <p>Hebrews 3–4 works the same passage even harder, quoting Psalm 95 and
        drawing the conclusion that "they were unable to enter because of unbelief"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 3:19">Hebrews 3:19</a>), followed
        immediately by <em>"let us fear lest any of you should seem to have failed to
        reach it"</em> (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 4:1">Hebrews 4:1</a>).</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Where Each Tradition Takes This</span>
          Everyone agrees the wilderness generation is a warning to the church; the
          disagreement is over what it proves. One reading: they show that
          covenant privileges without living faith save no one — the fall reveals
          what was never there. Another: they show that genuine covenant members can
          forfeit the inheritance. A third notes that the promise itself never
          failed — the nation still entered, in the next generation — so the passage
          concerns forfeiting a share in something that God nonetheless secures.
          Part III gives each of these its best hearing. For now, note only that the
          text is used by the New Testament as a live warning to believers, and that
          no responsible reading gets to make the warning hypothetical.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 7: REMNANT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="remnant">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Remnant — Israel Within Israel</h2>

        <p>From the prophets onward, the Old Testament develops a category that the
        assurance debate cannot do without: the <em>remnant</em> (<em>she'ar</em>,
        שְׁאָר). Not everyone inside the covenant community is, in the end, found to
        be among the people of God.</p>

        <ul>
          <li>Elijah is certain he is the only one left, and is told there are seven
          thousand who have not bowed to Baal
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Kings 19:18">1 Kings 19:18</a>). Note that
          Elijah could not see them — the true number was invisible to the most
          faithful prophet of his generation.</li>
          <li>Isaiah's son is named Shear-jashub, "a remnant shall return"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 7:3">Isaiah 7:3</a>), and the felled tree
          of Israel is said to keep a holy seed in its stump
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 6:13">Isaiah 6:13</a>).</li>
          <li>Micah, Zephaniah, Jeremiah, and Zechariah all speak of a preserved
          minority gathered after judgment.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Paul takes the category over wholesale in
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 9:6">Romans 9:6</a> — <em>"not all who are
        descended from Israel belong to Israel"</em> — and in
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 11:5">Romans 11:5</a>, where the seven
        thousand become "a remnant, chosen by grace."</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Visible and the Real</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The distinction between the covenant
          community as it appears and the covenant people as God knows them is not a
          Reformation invention. It is Isaiah's.</p>
          <p>This matters for assurance in two directions at once, and both are
          uncomfortable. It means membership, baptism, attendance, and reputation are
          not the same thing as belonging — which unsettles the presumptuous. It
          also means the community's judgment about a person is not final either,
          in either direction — which should quiet the person who has been written
          off by a congregation, and check the congregation doing the writing off.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 8: CIRCUMCISED HEART
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="heart">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Circumcised Heart</h2>

        <p>The Old Testament's own critique of merely external covenant membership is
        older and sharper than the church usually credits. The image is savage on
        purpose: the covenant sign cut into flesh is demanded of the heart.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Text</th><th>Who acts</th><th>What is said</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 10:16">Deut 10:16</a></td><td><strong>You</strong></td><td>"Circumcise therefore the foreskin of your heart, and be no longer stubborn"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 30:6">Deut 30:6</a></td><td><strong>God</strong></td><td>"The LORD your God will circumcise your heart... so that you will love him"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 4:4">Jer 4:4</a></td><td><strong>You</strong></td><td>"Circumcise yourselves to the LORD; remove the foreskin of your hearts"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 9:25-26">Jer 9:25–26</a></td><td>—</td><td>Judah is listed among the nations as "uncircumcised in heart," its physical sign counting for nothing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 36:26">Ezek 36:26</a></td><td><strong>God</strong></td><td>"I will give you a new heart... I will remove the heart of stone"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 2:28-29">Rom 2:28–29</a></td><td>—</td><td>"Circumcision is a matter of the heart, by the Spirit, not by the letter"</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Read the "who acts" column down the page. The command and the promise
        alternate, and Scripture never explains how they fit together. Deuteronomy
        says <em>do this</em> in chapter 10 and <em>God will do this</em> in chapter
        30. Jeremiah issues the command; Ezekiel records the promise. The two are
        held together by placement rather than by argument.</p>

        <p>Paul's conclusion in Romans 2 — that the real Jew is one inwardly, and the
        real circumcision is of the heart by the Spirit — is not a new doctrine
        imported into the Old Testament. It is a summary of it. He is quoting
        Deuteronomy back to people who had it memorised.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 9: EZEKIEL'S TWO WORDS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="ezekiel">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Ezekiel's Two Words</h2>

        <p>If you want the assurance tension in its sharpest Old Testament form, take
        it from a single prophet. Ezekiel says both of the following, and the church
        has never fully reconciled them.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          But when a righteous person turns away from his righteousness and does
          injustice and does the same abominations that the wicked person does, shall
          he live? None of the righteous deeds that he has done shall be remembered.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 18:24">Ezekiel 18:24</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          And I will put my Spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes
          and be careful to obey my rules.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 36:27">Ezekiel 36:27</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Chapter 18 is the great chapter of individual responsibility, written
        against a proverb the exiles were using to blame their fathers. Its whole
        argument is that a person's own present standing before God is genuinely
        theirs, genuinely reversible in both directions, and genuinely their
        responsibility. Chapter 36 promises that God will act on the will itself so
        that obedience follows — and, notably, says he will do it "not for your sake"
        but for the sake of his own name
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 36:22">36:22</a>).</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">An Observation Worth Carrying Forward</span>
          Ezekiel 18 is addressed to people who were <em>using doctrine to avoid
          responsibility</em> — "the fathers have eaten sour grapes and the children's
          teeth are set on edge." Ezekiel 36 is addressed to people in despair, who
          had concluded that their bones were dried up and their hope was lost
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 37:11">37:11</a>). The same prophet gives
          opposite-sounding words to opposite conditions. That is not
          inconsistency; it is pastoral aim. Part IV builds a good deal on this
          observation, because the two errors are still the two errors, and the
          medicine for one is poison to the other.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 10: HABAKKUK
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="habakkuk">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Habakkuk 2:4 — The Hinge</h2>

        <p>One Old Testament sentence is quoted three separate times in the New
        Testament, by two different authors, at the hinge of three different
        arguments. It is worth slowing down on.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Behold, his soul is puffed up; it is not upright within him, but the
          righteous shall live by his faith.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Habakkuk 2:4">Habakkuk 2:4</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>In context, Habakkuk has complained that God is using Babylon — a nation
        worse than Judah — to judge Judah, and has climbed his watchtower to wait for
        an answer. The answer is that the vision is for an appointed time and will
        not lie, that the proud will not endure, and that <em>the righteous will live
        by his 'emunah</em>. Given the setting, "faith" here carries its full Hebrew
        weight: the steady, holding-on trust of someone waiting for a promise that
        has not yet arrived. Habakkuk's righteous man is not making a decision; he is
        staying at his post.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Quotation</th><th>Argument it anchors</th><th>Emphasis</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 1:17">Romans 1:17</a></td><td>The gospel reveals a righteousness from God, received by faith</td><td><strong>Righteous</strong> — the basis of standing</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 3:11">Galatians 3:11</a></td><td>No one is justified by law, because the law is not "of faith"</td><td><strong>By faith</strong> — the mode of receiving</td></tr>
            <tr><td><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:38">Hebrews 10:38</a></td><td>Do not throw away your confidence; you have need of endurance</td><td><strong>Shall live</strong> — the continuance</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The Hebrews citation is the one usually left out of the sermon, and it is
        the most important for this study. Hebrews quotes the Greek version and
        <em>reverses the order of its two clauses</em>, so that the warning lands
        last:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          But my righteous one shall live by faith, and if he shrinks back, my soul
          has no pleasure in him.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:38">Hebrews 10:38</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Then the author immediately adds his own verdict: <em>"But we are not of
        those who shrink back and are destroyed, but of those who have faith and
        preserve their souls"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:39">10:39</a>) — and follows it with
        Hebrews 11, an entire chapter of people who kept going.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Verse That Refuses to Take Sides</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The single Old Testament text that the New
          Testament chose as its motto for justification by faith is also a text
          about not shrinking back.</p>
          <p>Whichever party quotes Habakkuk 2:4 as a knockdown argument is quoting
          half of it. Paul and the author of Hebrews are not using two different
          verses; they are using the same verse, and between them they cover the
          whole ground — a righteousness that is received, and a life that is lived
          out of it, over time, under pressure, before the vision arrives. That is
          the through line of this entire study, and it was written down by a minor
          prophet on a watchtower.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 11: NEW COVENANT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="newcovenant">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The New Covenant: God Undertakes the Human Side</h2>

        <p>The Old Testament ends without resolving its own problem. The law is good,
        the people cannot keep it, the curses have fallen, and the exile has happened.
        Into that failure the prophets speak of a covenant that will not work the way
        the old one did.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Behold, the days are coming, declares the LORD, when I will make a new
          covenant with the house of Israel... not like the covenant that I made with
          their fathers... my covenant that they broke, though I was their husband.
          But this is the covenant that I will make: I will put my law within them,
          and I will write it on their hearts... For I will forgive their iniquity,
          and I will remember their sin no more.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 31:31-34">Jeremiah 31:31–34</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Four things are promised, and every one of them bears on assurance.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">1</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Internalisation — the law written on the heart</div>
          <p>The problem with the old covenant is not diagnosed as a defect in the
          law but as a defect in the people: "they broke it." The remedy is not a
          lower standard; it is a relocated one. The commandment moves from tablets
          to the heart, which means obedience becomes something a person wants rather
          than only something they are told.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">2</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Universal knowledge — "they shall all know me"</div>
          <p>"No longer shall each one teach his neighbour... for they shall all know
          me, from the least of them to the greatest." Whatever else this means, it
          rules out a two-tier covenant people in which some members are inside the
          arrangement without knowing God at all. This is one of the strongest
          prophetic texts for the view that the new covenant community is, at its
          core, a regenerate one — and it is a genuinely difficult text for any view
          that treats new covenant membership as merely external.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">3</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Forgiveness that is final — "I will remember their sin no more"</div>
          <p>Not "overlook," not "postpone." Hebrews seizes on exactly this clause
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 10:17-18">Hebrews 10:17–18</a>) and draws
          the conclusion that where there is such forgiveness, there is no longer any
          offering for sin — meaning the sacrificial system has been closed, not that
          the believer's sin has become insignificant.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">4</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Divine causation — "I will cause you to walk"</div>
          <p>Ezekiel's parallel promise
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ezekiel 36:26-27">Ezekiel 36:26–27</a>) is more
          explicit still: a new heart, a new spirit, the heart of stone removed, God's
          own Spirit placed within, and the result — <em>"and cause you to walk in my
          statutes."</em> The obedience that the old covenant commanded, the new
          covenant produces.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Jeremiah then attaches a guarantee that is easy to miss because it is
        phrased as astronomy:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          If this fixed order departs from before me, declares the LORD, then shall
          the offspring of Israel cease from being a nation before me forever.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Jeremiah 31:36">Jeremiah 31:36</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The stability of the covenant is pinned to the stability of the sun and
        moon — that is, to something entirely outside the covenant partner's
        performance. This is the Old Testament's own deepest ground of assurance, and
        it is worth noticing where it is placed: immediately after the promise of
        forgiveness, and immediately before a description of a rebuilt city. Comfort
        is grounded in God's fixity, not in the people's improvement.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">The Honest Difficulty</span>
          If the new covenant includes "I will cause you to walk in my statutes" and
          "they shall all know me," why does the New Testament spend so much energy
          warning new covenant people not to fall away? That question is the engine
          of Part II and Part III, and no tradition answers it without cost. State it
          now and let it sit: <strong>the promises are unconditional in form, and the
          warnings addressed to the same people are unconditional in seriousness.</strong>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 12: THE THREAD
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="thread">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Thread, Stated</h2>

        <p>Pull the strands together and the shape of Old Testament salvation is
        remarkably stable across a thousand years of text.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Element</th><th>Old Testament</th><th>New Testament</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Who moves first</td><td>God seeks Adam; God remembers the covenant; God calls Abram out of Ur</td><td>"We love because he first loved us" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 4:19">1 Jn 4:19</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Basis of standing</td><td>Reckoned righteousness on the basis of trust (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 15:6">Gen 15:6</a>)</td><td>Justified by faith apart from works of law (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 3:28">Rom 3:28</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Means applied</td><td>Blood of the lamb on the doorpost; the whole sacrificial system</td><td>"Christ our Passover lamb has been sacrificed" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 5:7">1 Cor 5:7</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Order</td><td>Rescue, then law (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 20:2">Ex 20:2</a>)</td><td>Indicative, then imperative (Rom 1–11, then 12)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What is required</td><td><em>'Emunah</em> — trust that holds and therefore walks</td><td><em>Pistis</em> — "faith working through love" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:6">Gal 5:6</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Real danger</td><td>Wilderness generation; Ezekiel 18; exile</td><td>Hebrews 3–4, 6, 10; 1 Cor 10; the seven churches</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ground of comfort</td><td>God's name, oath, and the fixed order of sun and moon</td><td>Christ's finished work and intercession (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 7:25">Heb 7:25</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Deepest promise</td><td>A heart God himself circumcises; his Spirit within</td><td>The Spirit as seal and down payment (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:13-14">Eph 1:13–14</a>)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>There is no point in that table where the method changes. There is no
        dispensation of works followed by a dispensation of grace. There is one God
        rescuing one kind of people in one way, and asking of them, from the
        beginning, one thing described by a Hebrew word that means both faith and
        faithfulness because it never occurred to Hebrew to separate them.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Through Line</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">God saves by promise, applies the rescue by
          blood, credits righteousness to trust, requires of the trusting a loyalty
          he himself undertakes to produce, warns them in earnest that abandoning the
          covenant is fatal, and stakes the outcome on his own name.</p>
          <p>Every one of those clauses is Old Testament before it is New. The New
          Testament does not add a clause; it supplies the name of the lamb, the
          identity of the mediator, and the arrival of the Spirit who does the
          causing. <strong>What is required of the saved person has never
          changed.</strong> It is to hold on to the God who has hold of you — and to
          keep walking while the vision tarries.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Part II takes up the New Testament texts that seem to divide against each
        other on precisely this point, and reads each one in its own context before
        any harmonising is attempted.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Christ of the Covenants</cite>, O. Palmer Robertson. P&amp;R, 1980.
          <em>A standard covenant-theology treatment of the OT→NT continuity; clear on
          the structure of the covenants, and openly Reformed in its conclusions.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Old Testament Theology</cite>, John Goldingay. IVP, 2003–2009.
          <em>Three volumes; unusually careful about letting OT texts say what they say
          before Christian systematics arrives. Useful precisely where it resists tidy
          synthesis.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Covenant and Eschatology</cite> / <cite>God's Kingdom through God's Covenants</cite>,
          Peter Gentry and Stephen Wellum. Crossway, 2015 (abridged from
          <cite>Kingdom through Covenant</cite>). <em>Argues the "all shall know me"
          clause of Jeremiah 31 hard, in a direction that presses against
          infant-inclusive covenant models. The best statement of that case.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Epistle to the Hebrews</cite>, F. F. Bruce (NICNT, rev. 1990), on
          10:32–39. <em>The clearest short treatment of what Hebrews does with
          Habakkuk 2:4 and why the clause order matters.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

